{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And for the sacrifice of peace-offerings.</b> Corresponding to the entire Torah, as it is said: ״וכל נתיבותיה שלום״." "<b>Two cattle.</b> For it is written on two tablets." "<b>Five rams, five he-goats.</b> Corresponding to five commandments on this tablet and five on this tablet." "<b>Five lambs</b> corresponding to the five books of the Torah. And for all the other עתודים it is written defective [without vav], but this one is plene, because six things were renewed on that day: the first for priesthood, the first for princes, the first for sacrifices, the first for months, the first for the exodus from Egypt, the first for the prohibition of bamot. This I found. And it does not seem right to me, for in tractate Shabbat, chapter Rabbi Akiva, it concludes: that day took ten crowns, etc. Thus you learn that more than six things were renewed. Therefore it seems to me that the vav of קרבנו is needed to teach that one should not be arrogant. And the vav here alludes to six righteous men who issued from Nahshon, as explained above. And what is the reason they offered only a small amount — one bull, one ram? A parable: a king set out on a journey, and they would bring before him a meal suited to the road and suited to the inn. The king said to them: Is this how you honor me? They said to him: Our lord the king, on the road we give according to the road and according to the inn; but when you enter — when you enter with us into your palace — then you will see how much we honor you. So when the Tabernacle was erected, the princes offered only a little. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: This is My glory — in a small thing. They said before Him: Master of the Universe, we are standing in the wilderness, and according to the wilderness we offer before You; but when the Temple is built, speedily in our days, and You enter into Your palace — then You will see how many sacrifices and how many bulls we will offer before You. And thus it is written: ״הטיבה ברצונך את ציון״ etc. ״אז תחפוץ זבחי צדק עולה וכליל אז יעלו על מזבחך פרים״ — bulls [plural], and not one bull. And so you find with Solomon, when he inaugurated the Temple — what is written there? ״ויזבח שלמה את זבח השלמים בקר עשרים ושנים אלף וצאן מאה ועשרים אלך ויחנכו את בית ה׳״. And likewise in the days of Ezra it is written: ״והקריבו בית אלהא דנא תורין מאה דכרין מאתן אמרין ארבע מאה״."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:09:06.160657941Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}